01:09:26,250 --> 01:09:28,250
And if it's not an
ordinary, then it's

737
01:09:28,250 --> 01:09:32,630
usually a partial
differential equation,

738
01:09:32,630 --> 01:09:38,350
which involve a function that
is of more than one variable.

739
01:09:38,350 --> 01:09:42,050
So it will involve what's
called the partial derivative

740
01:09:42,050 --> 01:09:42,890
of the functions.

741
01:09:48,930 --> 01:10:08,770
So the unknown function
will be denoted by y.

742
01:10:08,770 --> 01:10:11,290
And the equation
that we're looking at

743
01:10:11,290 --> 01:10:19,210
is that we want some
particular value at, say, 0.

744
01:10:19,210 --> 01:10:25,610
And then we want the derivative
satisfying an equation.

745
01:10:25,610 --> 01:10:30,670
And the equation that we're
looking for is that we have--

746
01:10:34,730 --> 01:10:38,750
so f here and g are given.

747
01:10:38,750 --> 01:10:43,430
So think about those as
functions that are given.

748
01:10:43,430 --> 01:10:46,090
Those are two given functions.

749
01:10:46,090 --> 01:10:48,770
But they may not be super nice.

750
01:10:48,770 --> 01:10:53,210
But you know that
f is differentiable

751
01:10:53,210 --> 01:11:00,590
and the derivative is continuous
and g here is continuous.

752
01:11:03,290 --> 01:11:08,010
But in principle, you
know they are given.

753
01:11:08,010 --> 01:11:09,930
And now you're looking for--

754
01:11:09,930 --> 01:11:11,590
but it may be very,
very complicated.

755
01:11:11,590 --> 01:11:16,760
Maybe f is sine or
something like that.

756
01:11:16,760 --> 01:11:21,720
And so you're looking
for an unknown function y

757
01:11:21,720 --> 01:11:24,240
so that the derivative--
so you think

758
01:11:24,240 --> 01:11:27,840
about y as a function of x.

759
01:11:27,840 --> 01:11:31,480
And the derivative
of y is supposed

760
01:11:31,480 --> 01:11:36,960
to be equal to f but is now
applied to this unknown function

761
01:11:36,960 --> 01:11:40,240
and then plus g of x.

762
01:11:40,240 --> 01:11:43,560
So this here is much more tame,
but this is a bit complicated

763
01:11:43,560 --> 01:11:47,320
because it involves
the unknown function.

764
01:11:47,320 --> 01:11:52,160
And so we want to see--

765
01:11:52,160 --> 01:12:08,290
so we are asking the question,
does this ODE have a solution?

766
01:12:13,480 --> 01:12:24,410
And is the solution unique?

767
01:12:30,300 --> 01:12:32,920
So we're looking for a function.

768
01:12:32,920 --> 01:12:35,580
So here's what
we're looking for.

769
01:12:40,160 --> 01:12:46,600
We're looking for a function y
defined on some interval into R.

770
01:12:46,600 --> 01:12:49,300
This function has to
be differentiable.

771
01:12:49,300 --> 01:12:52,920
So this has to exist.

772
01:12:52,920 --> 01:13:02,080
A 0 has to be in that interval
because a is just a constant.

773
01:13:02,080 --> 01:13:17,250
And then we want, obviously,
y to satisfy this equation?

774
01:13:22,560 --> 01:13:24,560
That's what we're looking for.

775
01:13:24,560 --> 01:13:26,560
So this is existence.

776
01:13:26,560 --> 01:13:29,000
The thing is that
what we will prove

777
01:13:29,000 --> 01:13:33,940
is that there exists
some interval--

778
01:13:33,940 --> 01:13:35,540
so we will prove the following.

779
01:13:39,400 --> 01:13:42,400
So we prove the
following theorem,

780
01:13:42,400 --> 01:13:45,280
which has many different
names, and it is

781
01:13:45,280 --> 01:13:46,730
attributed to various people.

782
01:13:50,360 --> 01:13:52,220
But it's I guess.

783
01:13:57,200 --> 01:14:08,080
it's most often named the
Picard-Lindelöf theorem.

784
01:14:08,080 --> 01:14:18,920
And so this is that
given f and g as above,

785
01:14:18,920 --> 01:14:23,840
so f has to be differentiable
and the derivative continuous g

786
01:14:23,840 --> 01:14:25,300
just has to be continuous.

787
01:14:28,160 --> 01:14:43,880
Then for any a, there exists a
delta greater than 0 such that--

788
01:14:47,360 --> 01:14:54,480
and a solution to this
differential equation

789
01:14:54,480 --> 01:15:05,320
is ODE on the interval
from minus delta to delta.

790
01:15:05,320 --> 01:15:12,440
So we proved that there exists
a solution near the origin.

791
01:15:12,440 --> 01:15:15,960
The thing is that a
solution may blow up.

792
01:15:15,960 --> 01:15:19,580
So it may not exist on
a very long interval.

793
01:15:19,580 --> 01:15:21,540
It may just exist
on a small interval.

794
01:15:21,540 --> 01:15:25,200
It's all depending on f and g.

795
01:15:25,200 --> 01:15:28,880
But in particular,
will depend on f.

796
01:15:28,880 --> 01:15:42,000
And that moreover,
it is unique, meaning

797
01:15:42,000 --> 01:15:45,680
that if you take
another solution

798
01:15:45,680 --> 01:15:50,720
defined on the same interval,
then it must be that solution.

799
01:15:50,720 --> 01:16:00,080
And so now I just have a
minute to-- but we will

800
01:16:00,080 --> 01:16:02,120
prove this next time anyway.

801
01:16:02,120 --> 01:16:07,780
But I just want to, as
a teaser, if you will,

802
01:16:07,780 --> 01:16:19,480
I will just say that
the proof of this

803
01:16:19,480 --> 01:16:25,260
uses the contraction
mapping theorem.

804
01:16:35,460 --> 01:16:38,480
Maybe I will remind
you what it is.

805
01:16:38,480 --> 01:16:53,160
So recall that the
contraction mapping theorem

806
01:16:53,160 --> 01:16:54,671
is the following--

807
01:16:58,280 --> 01:17:03,560
that suppose you take a
metric space x and d--

808
01:17:03,560 --> 01:17:07,800
so D is the metric,
x is the space--

809
01:17:07,800 --> 01:17:12,030
and suppose you take a
map from x to itself.

810
01:17:15,200 --> 01:17:18,440
So then T is a map
from x to itself.

811
01:17:18,440 --> 01:17:25,590
T is said to be a
contracting map.

812
01:17:32,520 --> 01:17:37,560
If there exists some
constant, that constant--

813
01:17:37,560 --> 01:17:38,720
that's key.

814
01:17:38,720 --> 01:17:42,040
It has to be
strictly less than 1

815
01:17:42,040 --> 01:17:47,800
so that if you measure
distances between the images,

816
01:17:47,800 --> 01:17:52,560
so for any x and
y in capital X--

817
01:17:52,560 --> 01:17:56,440
this is for all x
and y in capital

818
01:17:56,440 --> 01:18:01,440
X. If you look at the distance
between the images of these two

819
01:18:01,440 --> 01:18:06,600
points, then it's less than
or equal to this constant that

820
01:18:06,600 --> 01:18:12,200
is strictly less
than 1, like this.

821
01:18:12,200 --> 01:18:15,760
So that's a contracting map.

822
01:18:15,760 --> 01:18:17,920
And the contracting
mapping theorem

823
01:18:17,920 --> 01:18:21,200
that we prove, I'll remind
you about it again next time,

824
01:18:21,200 --> 01:18:25,120
but just the contracting
mapping theorem

825
01:18:25,120 --> 01:18:27,760
that we proved is
that-- so we proved

826
01:18:27,760 --> 01:18:30,200
the following theorem, which
is a contracting mapping

827
01:18:30,200 --> 01:18:43,520
theorem, that if x here
is Cauchy complete,

828
01:18:43,520 --> 01:19:00,000
and T from x to x is
a contracting map,

829
01:19:00,000 --> 01:19:05,770
then T has a unique.

830
01:19:08,360 --> 01:19:09,220
fixed point.

831
01:19:14,940 --> 01:19:18,980
So there exists a unique
one-- often write like this--

832
01:19:18,980 --> 01:19:31,040
there exists one and only one
x0 so that this map takes x0

833
01:19:31,040 --> 01:19:31,820
to itself.

834
01:19:31,820 --> 01:19:34,520
So that's a contraction
mapping theorem.

835
01:19:34,520 --> 01:19:39,800
And so it seems
like what does ODE

836
01:19:39,800 --> 01:19:42,920
has to do with them,
this abstract concept

837
01:19:42,920 --> 01:19:44,760
of a metric space?

838
01:19:44,760 --> 01:19:48,400
So it's tied
together using this.

839
01:19:48,400 --> 01:19:52,320
And then the space x, it's not--

840
01:19:55,920 --> 01:19:59,000
you may think that that's close,
but it's not quite the case.

841
01:19:59,000 --> 01:20:01,960
You may think that it's
like a continuous function

842
01:20:01,960 --> 01:20:04,280
on an interval like this.

843
01:20:04,280 --> 01:20:07,680
That's close.

844
01:20:07,680 --> 01:20:09,280
You may think that
because that's

845
01:20:09,280 --> 01:20:11,640
a space we've talked
about a lot, this

846
01:20:11,640 --> 01:20:13,320
here, with this metric d.

847
01:20:13,320 --> 01:20:15,920
We talked about with
this maximum distance.

848
01:20:15,920 --> 01:20:17,860
It's not actually
quite the case.

849
01:20:17,860 --> 01:20:22,200
It's a little bit more
complicated than this.

850
01:20:22,200 --> 01:20:25,360
But x is related to
this because x here

851
01:20:25,360 --> 01:20:33,680
will be a ball around the
function that is constant,

852
01:20:33,680 --> 01:20:39,200
the function that is
constant a in this space.

853
01:20:39,200 --> 01:20:44,080
And we have to choose
this R carefully.

854
01:20:44,080 --> 01:20:46,600
And so that's a space x.

855
01:20:46,600 --> 01:20:49,940
And now I haven't defined
the function the map g.

856
01:20:49,940 --> 01:20:51,500
But we'll do that next time.

857
01:20:51,500 --> 01:20:54,080
And I will remind
you about this also.

858
01:20:54,080 --> 01:20:56,310
Any questions?

